Delabella's exact signs (patch 1070) without the heap and with fewer
components where the points are near each other (mitcad#884). The signs
and so the triangulations are the same; only the time differs.

The case: a perforated plate (a plane face with 1,187 round holes, 172,924
boundary nodes), whose base triangulation took 1.1 s in Delabella. Of its
14 million in-circle tests 334,000 (the points of one hole are nearly
cocircular) reach the exact evaluation, which built every expansion in a
std::vector: dozens of heap allocations per test, about half of
Delabella's time.
The face's triangulation is the same before and after (each triangle's
nodes compared); Delabella's time on it falls from 1.1 s to 0.49 s.

- Expansions are held in place (Expansion<N>, N the most components the
  operation can give), with the same operations in the same order as
  before (grow, sum, scale, multiply, negate, difference).
- The exact evaluations (orientExact, incircleExactDifferences,
  incircleExactAny) are not inlined, so that their frames (the expansions
  in place, tens of kilobytes) are set up only when the filter cannot
  decide; inlined, every in-circle test set one up.
- incircleSign: when the six differences of the points are exact in
  floating point (as between near points), the determinant is evaluated
  from them directly: (adx^2 + ady^2) bc + (bdx^2 + bdy^2) ca +
  (cdx^2 + cdy^2) ab, each product exact (Shewchuk's stage B, here exact
  because no difference is rounded). Otherwise the expansions of the
  differences as before.
- The faces made once the hull is no longer flat get only the sign of
  their normal's z (Face::turn): Triangulate() reads nothing else of them.
  Their normal's x and y, in long double, are left out.

Upstream: new.
